# NOT MOUNTED, NOT DEPLOYED. A reference copy of the one site block this stack
# needs when it shares a box with another project's Caddy.
#
# ## Where the real copy lives, and why it is not here
#
# On the shared box, `<neighbour>/Caddyfile` is a bind mount from **the neighbour's** deploy
# tree, so anyone who deploys the neighbour — a person, or an agent told "push the server
# changes" — writes that whole file from the neighbour's git. A block that existed only on
# the box would be deleted by the next neighbour deploy. Caddy would reload
# **cleanly**, this backend would stop resolving, and the neighbour's deploy would report
# success with nothing in either repo to explain it.
#
# That is not a hypothetical either: this project already had exactly that
# happen once. `<translate host>` was hand-added
# to the box on 30 Jul 2026 and existed in no repository for two weeks.
#
# So the block below has to be committed to **the neighbour's** repo, in its
# `infra/vps/Caddyfile`, and deployed from there. This copy exists so that this
# repo documents its own hostname rather than pointing at a private one.
#
#     # in the NEIGHBOUR's repo, not this one
#     ssh ... 'cat <neighbour>/Caddyfile' > /tmp/caddyfile.box
#     diff /tmp/caddyfile.box infra/vps/Caddyfile     # merge anything the box has
#     # ...append the block below, commit, then:
#     tar -czf - -C infra/vps Caddyfile | ssh ... 'tar -xzf - -C <neighbour deploy dir>'
#     ssh ... 'cd <neighbour deploy dir> && docker compose exec caddy caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile'
#     ssh ... 'cd <neighbour deploy dir> && docker compose restart caddy'
#
# **Validate then restart — `caddy reload` does not work on that box.** Reload is
# the admin API: it POSTs the new config to :2019, and the neighbour's Caddyfile sets
# `admin off`, so nothing is listening and it fails with `connection refused`.
# Validate needs no admin API, and it is what makes the restart safe: a malformed
# Caddyfile makes Caddy refuse to **start**, which takes TLS for every hostname
# on the box with it.
#
# ## Before it is deployed
#
#   1. An EXPLICIT A record for pb.openscrgen.app -> <BOX_IP>. The zone carries a
#      wildcard, so without one the challenge is answered by whatever the
#      wildcard points at, this Caddy never sees it, and no certificate is ever
#      issued. On a proxying provider it must also be unproxied; the zone this
#      uses is on Vercel's nameservers, which do not proxy. See README.md.
#   2. /opt/openscreengen up, so `openscreengen-pocketbase` is resolvable on that network.
#
# Deploying ahead of either is not fatal — Caddy serves every other site
# normally and retries in the background — but enough failed ACME attempts in an
# hour reaches a Let's Encrypt rate limit for that one name.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Open Screenshot Generator's community feed (repo: open-screenshot-generator).
#
# `openscreengen-pocketbase`, never `pocketbase`. Compose registers a service's own name as
# a DNS alias on every network it joins, so a second service called `pocketbase`
# visible to this Caddy would give two claimants for that name — Docker hands
# back both records and round-robins them, and roughly half of <neighbour PocketBase host>'s
# requests would be answered by this database instead.
pb.openscrgen.app {
	# Realtime subscriptions are Server-Sent Events on this one path, and a
	# buffered proxy turns a live feed into nothing until the buffer fills. Caddy
	# does detect text/event-stream on its own; this is explicit because the
	# failure mode is silence rather than an error.
	#
	# The app does not subscribe today. The route is here so that turning it on
	# is a client change rather than a server deploy.
	handle /api/realtime {
		reverse_proxy openscreengen-pocketbase:8090 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy openscreengen-pocketbase:8090
	}

	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
		-Server
	}

	# NO Access-Control-Allow-Origin here. PocketBase sends its own (`*` by
	# default, and the app sends no cookies, only a bearer token), and two copies
	# of that header is an error rather than a hint: the browser rejects the
	# response outright and every request in the feed fails CORS.

	log {
		output file /data/access-openscreengen.log {
			roll_size 10MiB
			roll_keep 5
		}
	}
}
